\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A Solitant priest hears the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and thinks of the pure white glow of Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship. Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of sparkling salt pans, or sun-bleached bone. As it is with white, so it is with words, they mean different things to different people. It is some wonder then, that Solraheen would choose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world. He dwelt in the void, spoke one word, the world emerged, and people haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s not how its supposed to }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 be,}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 Vigram said, and such chocking the words.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m asking you to consider\u8230\'85consider this: Did Solraheen choose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant? I\u8217\'92ll answer: no. This is false. Words are fixed. Or rather, they used to be fixed. Take the word \u8216\'91art\u8217\'92 for example. When Solraheen walked the earth, an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 deliver old Solitant sermon titled \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91Art as Exegetical Instrument\u8217\'92. Vigram claimed that he didn\u8217\'92t normally go into the temple, because that meant he\u8217\'92d have to listen to a dullard speak, but he\u8217\'92d gone, and now he was leaning forward, elbows on the table, using his hands to communicate because all of this was crucial. These were delicate gestures, as if he were subjecting Mandri to a kind of surgery where the degeneracy of this fallen world was being extracted from his mind by means of interrogation.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri had heard it all before. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 What is art?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who is an artist?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who isn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she\u8217\'92s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? Give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, and what does he do? The standard delivery of the sermon leaves the derivation of the answer as an exercise to the audience. For the prude, the answer was a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch, just like the real thing. And to place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 well! As Vigram will attest, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 that}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  is an affront to the sacred.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t mean to interrupt,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93But, maybe we should begin the lesson?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, no,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, waving him off. \u8220\'93I should mention that we\u8217\'92re waiting for a third.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Who?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram took a long inhale, as if the absence of this mysterious third weighed on him. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93She prefers to introduce herself,\u8221\'94 he finally said. \u8220\'93She should be here shortly. She is often waylaid. In the meantime, I\u8217\'92ve some food coming, and I hoped to take this opportunity to get to know each other. It takes people a while to get comfortable being around me. I\u8217\'92m a difficult man, I know it. But, I need you to feel comfortable, because I need to know who you are. You see, I\u8217\'92m looking for a very specific type of person. A painter, yes, but I require a painter who fits me like a glove, as they say. \u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 One might be led to believe that He chose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant. But this is false, or so the Solitants claim. Words are fixed. The word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91art\u8217\'92, for example, originally had a clear meaning. Back when Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The room was full of subtleties which could not be captured in a painting. The cool, dark grays called for a shade of blue which he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d never been able to find, or mix. The blackwood floor creaked under his chair, and that sound, that tactile sense of something old expiring in the humidity, that could only be hinted at in an image. Two windows set into the long wall behind him sent breakfast sunbeams slanting across the sturdy dining table. That light was alive with dust motes fleeing his every breath, and good luck painting the sensation of causing a stir. Then there was the ceiling, which sagged and cracked, as though the plastered clay had given up. The sense of dread that sag elicited could only be felt if one was sitting underneath it and looking up.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You don\u8217\'92t agree?\u8221\'94 Vigram asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram sat at the head of the table, fat hands folded over his belly beard.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, no, I was just thinking,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, hoping that was enough to waylay any suspicion that he hadn\u8217\'92t been listening. Vigram had been ranting about the degeneracy of culture, and if he were to listen, he would want to say things, and that would inevitably lead to an argument. An argument was to be avoided, because he was eager to make a good first impression. But he wasn\u8217\'92t good at feigning attention either, not while Vigram bloviated all over the table. Apparently, everyone else in the world was too stupid to understand that the world was better some five thousand years ago.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92 jowels sort of flapped while he spoke. The man was big and tall, burly, with a broad face, and dark, tufted brows. Fiftyish years old. There was a polite smile tucked inside his beard whenever he wasn\u8217\'92t speaking, but it was not sincere.  Already, he was rumbling through another bold statement about how artists, in this age, were all invalids.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t mean to interrupt,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93But, maybe we should begin the lesson?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, no,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, waving him off. \u8220\'93I should mention that we\u8217\'92re waiting for a third.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Who?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram took a long inhale, as if the absence of this mysterious third weighed on him. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93She prefers to introduce herself,\u8221\'94 he finally said. \u8220\'93She should be here shortly. She is often waylaid. In the meantime, I\u8217\'92ve some food coming, and I hoped to take this opportunity to get to know each other. It takes people a while to get comfortable being around me. I\u8217\'92m a difficult man, I know it. But, I need you to feel comfortable, because I need to know who you are. You see, I\u8217\'92m looking for a very specific type of person. A painter, yes, but I require a painter who fits me like a glove, as they say. \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 At least ten other artists had tried to teach Vigram how to paint, and they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d all been fired. It was Mandri\u8217\'92s turn today. He\u8217\'92d come because he needed the money, but there was also a little motivating curiosity, because why had all the others failed, or given up, when the money was so good? A few, it was rumored, were so discouraged by the time of their final lesson, that they packed their bags and took off for the other side of the world, never to be seen again. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 As it turns out, there was no great mystery: no one wanted to teach Vigram because he was absolutely riddled with opinions. And the man wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t some skinny person with a scholar\u8217\'92s neck, one of those people with a mind so deep that they forget to eat and so you could be sure that they have interesting things to say. Vigram had probably never missed a meal in his life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m speaking about }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 words}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 ,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93They\u8217\'92ve been deconstructed by this degenerate culture that we\u8217\'92re all swimming in. Consider the word \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 for example. What would you say, \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 means?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You mean the color?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Of course I mean the color!\u8221\'94 Vigram snipped. He pounded his table with his fat hand, and one of his slaves popped out of the kitchen. The slave carried two steaming plates of Meskalimi leech, and then another came, ferrying more incomprehensible plates and bowls of leech.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93White means the pure color \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93Any and all variation in that meaning is degeneracy.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He picked up a spoon and stirred his leechy porridge. He took one bite of it, then immediately clutched his jaw and winced. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ve a troublesome tooth,\u8221\'94 he said before spitting the glob of unswallowed mush back into its bowl. Then he sighed, because nothing on the table could accommodate toothache. This was his leech farm, and so every dish\u8217\'92s principal ingredient was some variation of the Meskalimi leech. This particular leech was a prized delicacy throughout the Hrkan world, as the meat, if it can be called meat, cured infertility, and somehow, fertility as well. It was also quite sweet, and chewy. Each bite forced the tongue into acrobatics. On Vigram\u8217\'92s second bite, his tongue slipped, and the pain had him talking out the side of his cheek in an odd drawl. And now it hurt to hear the man speak, in more ways than one.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t understand,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. \u8220\'93}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mandri, the conflict over meaning is the very source of all conflict. Consider this: a Solitant priest hears the word \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and thinks of the pure white glow of Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship. Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of daggering down those plain and stainless robes. As it is with white, so it is with words, they mean different things to different people. And now, consider\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 why did Solraheen chose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world? He dwelt in the void, spoke one word, the world emerged, and people haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Is that not how it happened?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Of course that\u8217\'92s how it happened!\u8221\'94 Vigram said with a knitted brow. \u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m asking you to consider\u8230\'85consider this: Did Solraheen choose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant? I\u8217\'92ll answer: no. This is false. Words are fixed. Or rather, they used to be fixed. Take the word \u8216\'91art\u8217\'92 for example. When Solraheen walked the earth, an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri chowed down on the cold, crumbled chunks of what he supposed was boiled leech. It was quite bitter on account of the overpowering sauce, but the effort to suppress a gag helped keep him from speaking his mind. He smiled and nodded and oh-reallyed as Vigram went on to deliver an old Solitant sermon titled \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91Art as Exegetical Instrument\u8217\'92. Vigram claimed that he didn\u8217\'92t normally go into the temple, because that meant he\u8217\'92d have to listen to a dullard speak, but he\u8217\'92d gone, and now he was leaning forward, elbows on the table, using his hands to communicate because all of this was crucial. These were delicate gestures, as if he were subjecting Mandri to a kind of surgery where the degeneracy of this fallen world was being extracted from his mind by means of interrogation.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri had heard it all before. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 What is art?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who is an artist?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who isn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she\u8217\'92s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? Give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, and what does he do? The standard delivery of the sermon leaves the derivation of the answer as an exercise to the audience. For the prude, the answer was a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch, just like the real thing. And to place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 well! As Vigram will attest, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 that}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  is an affront to the sacred.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why not choose a different word?\u8221\'94 Mandri suggested as he dug into a bowl of mush. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because words must have walls,\u8221\'94 Vigram said as he pushed his own plate away. \u8220\'93The true artist, I say this, the true artist is a divine instrument. They must be kept apart from the self-skinny horde. Though, I }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 would}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  like to return to the Old Jandri word for \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91artist\u8217\'92, but unfortunately, that word is lost to time. There is only the word: \u8216\'91bursheg\u8217\'92. It means something like \u8216\'91fertile ground\u8217\'92, but in the Solitant tradition it means \u8216\'91true artist\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Interesting,\u8221\'94 Mandri lied.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ll tell you what\u8217\'92s interesting,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, lifting his thick brows. \u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ve heard someone say that art has nothing to do with what a person makes. Have you ever heard that? I didn\u8217\'92t understand it at first, but you know, it\u8217\'92s true. True artistry, consider this, true artistry is a state of mind. Some of the greatest artists never made anything at all.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri stuffed his mouth with a breadroll in a futile attempt to stifle himself. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I was under the impression,\u8221\'94 he said as he worked the roll into his cheek, \u8220\'93that an artist makes art. I mean, that seems like the most straightforward definition to me. I mean, if we\u8217\'92re getting upset about people twisting words, then\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram frowned and tutted. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Nobody makes anything,\u8221\'94 he insisted. \u8220\'93We can only ever rearrange. When you paint, you push pigment around. How is that any different than my footprints rearranging sand? No, either everyone is an artist, or, and this is my claim, an artist is a certain type}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 of}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 person. A certain type of }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 mind}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . You know, too many big ideas, too many shallow excuses, that sort of thing. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a mind marked by excess\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 not abundance\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 excess}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 .\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t look at me,\u8221\'94 Mandri said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram smiled. The fat swerve of his bottom lip was a baited hook. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So exaggerated is the artist\u8217\'92s sense of self that it becomes impossible to describe them without resorting to sarcasm.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re still looking at me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s smile swerved. \u8220\'93There is no one else here.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Two slaves stood at attention by the kitchen\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s double-swing doors. Vigram\u8217\'92s blindness to their existence was cruel, but this did not dismantle his point. Mandri did think highly of himself.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Why shouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t he?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 When he was ten, he could paint a portrait in oils that rivaled the old Jeketian masters. These weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t polish-jobs where the paint was thin and every hair was rendered into being like he\u8217\'92d lost the plot of reality. By his hand, a single stroke of umber was enough to define the chestnut skin, the high-arched cheek, the skull underneath, the light, the shadow, even the mood of the piece. Warms and cools in one single stroke! With twenty-four, he could find a vivid likeness. There might be a few extra nibbles to make a beauty\u8217\'92s eyes glisten and her cleavage heave, but nobody counted those. Well, men like Vigram counted them. Lascivious men counted them. Probably, every man counted them. Some women, presumably.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And like any prideful artist, Mandri would strut around the grand Hrkan galleries and make clear his contempt for his contemporaries. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d stand in front of a painting and say, \u8220\'93This piece really }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 moves}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  me,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d make a show of walking away. All must bear witness to his mop of black hair as it flounced itself in time with his skinny, stiff-backed step. Or he\u8217\'92d say, \u8220\'93I weep at every stroke,\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d flash a knowing smile. Back in those days, he\u8217\'92d say these things directly to a fellow artist\u8217\'92s face if he could get to it. He never thought of it as bullying because artists, like himself, were safe targets for playful derision; it wasn\u8217\'92t the person he was making fun of, it was their failed attempts to express themselves, and those were }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 completely}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  different things. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d never go after the person.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 No, never.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Artists were the best people. They needed tough skin, unearned confidence, and a flair for the dramatic. Without any of that, they expire in obscurity. Then there is the servility. Above all, an artist must be servile to the hand that feeds. And look at that, Vigram had a lot of food at his table. And here he was, eating it. And that meant he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d become the one thing he\u8217\'92d swore he\u8217\'92d never be: an employee.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He looked to Vigram, who had been babbling about artistry in the \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91classical sense\u8217\'92, as could only be found in the \u8216\'91uncorrupted ages\u8217\'92.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram,\u8221\'94 Mandri said confidently, \u8220\'93you are an idiot.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram choked on a wad of smushed leech. He coughed, pounded his chest, then coughed again. Once recovered, he glared at Mandri with his protuberant eyes, oblivious to the brown specks of food in his long beard. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93In whose house do we sit?!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yours.\u8221\'94 Mandri said as he mustered a disarming smile. \u8220\'93But when it comes to these things, when it comes to matters of art, I am correct. If I am not correct, then you certainly aren\u8217\'92t.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why? Because I like some painters who aren\u8217\'92t you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t care who you like. And to be honest, I\u8217\'92m don\u8217\'92t really consider myself to be a painter.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93If you\u8217\'92re not a painter, then why am I paying you for a lesson?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93This lesson is a charity.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But I am going to }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 pay}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  you!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Even so.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Leech juice dripped down the corner of Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s lip, accenting his frown. He wiped it away, then broke into a jolly smile. \u8220\'93Hah!\u8221\'94 he chortled as he stabbed his clam shell into a bowl of fermented leech. So much lard was mushed into those leeches that the gray paste could grease a wheel. Vigram\u8217\'92s outstretched tongue batted at the stringy slop before he slumped it all down his gullet. He patted his fat lips dry with his fifth napkin, then threw it onto the floor to join the others.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We both know,\u8221\'94 he said, \u8220\'93that you\u8217\'92ve only come here for the money.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then we are both mistaken.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram rolled his eyes and set his leech lard mush aside. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Listen,\u8221\'94 he said. He put on his business face. \u8220\'93I should say that I am aware of you\u8217\'92re history.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93My history?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes. I\u8217\'92ve been told that you\u8217\'92ve given up on your talent, that you haven\u8217\'92t painted a thing in years. I\u8217\'92ve also heard that you bought a farm and it failed. Life is not going well, is it?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t complain. Some have it worse.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But why have you given up the craft? Success was there for you. In this degenerate world, you might have done some good. Did something untoward happen?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, nothing like that,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. He fumbled around for something to say, because he would never explain this aspect of himself to anyone. \u8220\'93I suppose,\u8221\'94 he said, \u8220\'93I didn\u8217\'92t want to debase myself by doing what it takes to succeed.\u8221\'94}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Debase yourself!?\u8221\'94 Vigram chortled. \u8220\'93What a thing to say! Who cares about debasement in this dreary world? We\u8217\'92re already at the bottom. Success is success, by whatever means. Look at me\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I should tell you how I got my start: I murdered my own father to inherit four ounces of silver.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s ears perked up. \u8220\'93Did you really?\u8221\'94 He studied Vigram closely, tried to find that sincere smile hiding in Vigram\u8217\'92s beard.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That was only the first debasement,\u8221\'94 Vigram said plainly. \u8220\'93The second was to loan those four ounces out to a leech farm. Of all places, a }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 leech}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  farm.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93About that,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ve been meaning to ask, why a leech farm?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram leaned back in his chair and made himself comfortable. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Beyond the blunt symbolism of the leech, there wasn\u8217\'92t much of a reason. I chose this particular farm because it supplied a Solitant temple with leeches for their bloodletting rituals. That meant low risk, because temples are always the last thing the mob will torch in lean times. It wasn\u8217\'92t long until the lean times hit especially hard, when the only bread available was made from fern-roots, and that\u8217\'92s when this farm\u8217\'92s former proprietors felt the squeeze. They asked for leniency on the interest rate. I didn\u8217\'92t budge. I took over the farm. The leechmen accused me of committing usury against Solraheen Himself, because in some small way I was, but Solraheen is dead and so what does it matter? I made bold with a priest and prayed about it around a jug of beer. I flashed him a slip of silver, and the bleary shine blinded him to all my iniquity.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m sorry,\u8221\'94 Mandri said frankly. \u8220\'93If you\u8217\'92re feeling like I\u8217\'92ve insulted you with that debasement comment, I didn\u8217\'92t mean it that way. I was talking about myself, my own path.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, no,\u8221\'94 Vigram said with a calming hand. \u8220\'93I took no offense. I\u8217\'92m simply being direct with you. Remember, I want you to feel comfortable around me. People can sense if I wear a mask, and they clam up. You see, I\u8217\'92m not a good person. I should say that I am a good person in the original sense, but I\u8217\'92m not \u8216\'91good\u8217\'92, not in the degenerate sense that people mean.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I see.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, It gets worse. You see, with the farm in hand, I lent smaller amounts over shorter terms to farmers who needed emergency loans to pay their taxes after droughts or infestations. I split these debts up and sold them off in Meskalim\u8217\'92s bond market. There\u8217\'92s not much money in Meskalim itself, but it has a busy port and that means access to all the important markets of the Hrkan world. From there, it was a simple matter of reducing my eyes to tiny pinpricks, ridding myself of limbs, segmenting the body, and now that I\u8217\'92d transformed into a leech, I attached myself }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 firmly}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  to the continent\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s rump and drank my fill. Anyone who tried to shake me off before I\u8217\'92d finished become my slave. I picked out the salvageable fools for my own use. That\u8217\'92s how I managed to round up a brood of leeches to pour over lists of sums credited and debts owed. So long as I maintain a layer of abstraction between these creatures and the brutes who collect my debts, it\u8217\'92s all self-sustaining. Now, I have a comfortable life. Idle hours. I should say, the debasement was worth every effort.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram licked his }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 plump fingers clean of sun-dried leech oil. This oil was a true delicacy, squeezed as it was from the salivary glands of leeches aged twenty years or more. Leeches that big are more devourers than bloodsuckers, so they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re much more expensive to feed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Now,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 You know all about the making of a painting. That\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s why I respect you by default. But I can see how you hide your willfullness. That is a bad sign for partnership. But, you are here, and you are a little desperate for money, so that is a good sign..\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Again,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m not here for the money.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then why walk all this way?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 Because?}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t need a reason to do whatever I do. I do it. I\u8217\'92m here. That\u8217\'92s all.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram jaw slackened. For the first time he seemed to deeply consider something Mandri said. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Are you saying you\u8217\'92re aimless?\u8221\'94 he asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No,\u8221\'94 Mandri answered. \u8220\'93All I\u8217\'92m saying is that I\u8217\'92m here. And you know who isn\u8217\'92t?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Who?\u8221\'94 Vigram replied, intrigued.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Your friend who prefers to introduce herself.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ah,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, relaxing. \u8220\'93Yes. It is as I said\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 she is often waylaid. But you\u8217\'92re right, we\u8217\'92ve waited long enough.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram clapped his meaty hands, prompting one of the nameless slaves to come and take his plates. Once done, he stood up. His chair protested the sudden shift of his massive weight with a terrible squeak.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We will begin,\u8221\'94 he said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri nodded, then wiped his own mouth and excused himself from the table. He took his time, because he wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t looking forward to beginning anything. But, he had to. He needed the money. He needed it quite badly. He just didn\u8217\'92t want to tell Vigram what it was for, or else the man might try to intervene.}}